---
import AlgoPagina from "../../components/AlgoPagina.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import SiteNav from "../../components/SiteNav.astro";
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { TrocoModos } from "../../components/TrocoLab.tsx";
import { Predicao } from "../../components/Predicao.tsx";
---

<Base domain="algorithms" title="Greedy, and the case it misses: making change — OpenLabs" description="Pay an amount with the fewest coins. Greedy takes the biggest coin that fits; dynamic programming fills a table of every smaller amount. With Brazilian coins they agree; with the coins Britain used before 1971 they do not.">
<SiteNav current="labs" />
<AlgoPagina
  titulo="Greedy, and the case it misses: making change"
  href="labs/greedy-change"
  nota={["Greedy: decide now, never look back.", "The table: solve every smaller case first.", "Same question, two prices, one of them always right."]}
  quebre={[
    { b: "Pick Toy coins {1, 3, 4} and pay 6.", texto: "Greedy takes 4, then 1, then 1: three coins. The table finds 3 + 3: two. Taking the biggest coin first locked greedy out of the better answer." },
    { b: "Pick Britain before 1971 and pay 48 pence.", texto: "Greedy pays 30 + 12 + 6. The optimum is 24 + 24. Real coin systems were not always greedy-friendly — the modern ones are designed to be." },
    { b: "Pick the Brazilian real and slide the amount.", texto: "Greedy and the table agree everywhere up to 500. The system is canonical: for these coins, the cheap rule is also the right one." },
    { b: "Type 5 7 and pay 10.", texto: "Greedy grabs 7 and is stuck with 3. The table pays 5 + 5. Some amounts — 1, 2, 3 — cannot be paid at all, and the table marks them." },
  ]}
  erros={[
    { crenca: "Greedy algorithms are approximations.", verdade: "Some are exact: Dijkstra, Kruskal's and Prim's minimum spanning trees, and coin change with canonical systems. Being greedy is only wrong when an early choice can block a better later one." },
    { crenca: "Dynamic programming is a kind of recursion.", verdade: "It is recursion that remembers. Each amount is solved once and stored; the table here fills from 0 upward and never solves the same amount twice." },
    { crenca: "The optimal answer is always much more expensive.", verdade: "The table costs amount × coins steps — 18 for {1, 3, 4} and 6 — against greedy's 3. More work, but polynomial, not exponential like trying every combination." },
  ]}
  fronteira="Amounts go up to 500 and systems up to 8 coin values, with an unlimited supply of each coin. The table answers the fewest-coins question; counting the number of different ways to make change is a different dynamic programme. The canonical check only looks up to 500, which the lab says rather than claiming a proof."
  ligacoes={[
    { href: "labs/shortest-path", texto: "Four ways across a city — Dijkstra, the greedy algorithm that is right" },
    { href: "labs/how-work-grows", texto: "Six ways to grow — why the table beats trying every subset" },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    Pay an amount using as few coins as possible. The obvious rule is <b>greedy</b>:
    take the biggest coin that fits, subtract, repeat. It looks at each coin value once
    and never reconsiders.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sometimes that is exactly right, and sometimes it is quietly wrong. The other way is
    <b>dynamic programming</b>: solve every smaller amount first — 1, 2, 3, … — each one
    from answers already in the table, and read the answer for the amount you want. It
    is more work, but it never misses. This lab puts both side by side and finds, for
    any coin system you type, the smallest amount where greedy loses.
  </p>
  <TrocoModos slot="palco" client:only="react" />
  <Predicao
    slot="predicao"
    client:visible
    pergunta="Coins are 1, 3 and 4. What is the smallest amount where greedy uses more coins than necessary?"
    opcoes={["6", "5", "7"]}
    correta={0}
    revelacao="Up to 5 greedy is fine (5 = 4 + 1, two coins, and no single coin is 5). At 6 greedy takes 4 + 1 + 1 while 3 + 3 uses two coins. The lab finds this automatically for any system: try it."
  />
</AlgoPagina>
<SiteFooter />
</Base>
